1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,362
In this video I take the template and map a real
process: the annual leave request at AI Caravan

2
00:00:06,362 --> 00:00:09,056
Limited, the same example
we use in the slides.

3
00:00:09,056 --> 00:00:12,957
Watch how each step lands in the
lane of the person who does it.

4
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:16,877
I've opened a copy of the template and
zoomed out so the whole page fits.

5
00:00:16,877 --> 00:00:21,741
First, the lanes: the employee, the
line manager and the HR coordinator.

6
00:00:21,741 --> 00:00:26,675
Then the two systems: the HR portal
and the manager's own team sheet.

7
00:00:28,533 --> 00:00:30,158
Every map starts with the trigger.

8
00:00:30,158 --> 00:00:35,534
I select the trigger in the legend, press Command
D to copy it, drag the copy into the employee

9
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:38,610
lane, and type what starts the
process, with its touch time.

10
00:00:40,067 --> 00:00:41,205
Then the steps.

11
00:00:41,205 --> 00:00:45,280
One box per step, a verb and an object,
in the lane of whoever does it.

12
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:48,751
The line manager reads the
email and checks for clashes.

13
00:00:48,751 --> 00:00:49,819
Six minutes.

14
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,487
Connect the two and type
the wait on the arrow.

15
00:00:53,487 --> 00:00:56,819
The email sits in the inbox for
a day before anyone reads it.

16
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:01,789
Checking the team sheet is a lookup, not a
step, so it goes in the team sheet lane with a

17
00:01:01,789 --> 00:01:02,544
dashed border.

18
00:01:02,544 --> 00:01:06,306
Command E opens the style,
and I add dashed equals one.

19
00:01:12,267 --> 00:01:13,916
Same pattern for the next steps.

20
00:01:13,916 --> 00:01:17,631
The manager emails HR for the balance:
two minutes, then a day of waiting.

21
00:01:17,631 --> 00:01:20,998
HR looks it up in the portal
and replies: four minutes.

22
00:01:26,933 --> 00:01:30,857
Now the decision: is the balance
enough, and is there no clash?

23
00:01:30,857 --> 00:01:32,808
Every decision needs two exits.

24
00:01:32,808 --> 00:01:36,070
No, and the employee
gets a decline by email.

25
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:39,007
Yes, and the manager replies, approved.

26
00:01:41,733 --> 00:01:43,509
Then the part nobody sees.

27
00:01:43,509 --> 00:01:47,921
The employee types the same
request again into the HR portal.

28
00:01:47,921 --> 00:01:52,739
HR approves it there, the portal sends a
confirmation, and the manager updates the team sheet

29
00:01:52,739 --> 00:01:53,529
by hand.

30
00:02:02,133 --> 00:02:05,314
Now tag the pain points,
copying tags from the legend.

31
00:02:05,314 --> 00:02:07,230
Waiting for the manager is WAIT.

32
00:02:07,230 --> 00:02:09,076
Reading the email, READ.

33
00:02:09,076 --> 00:02:11,525
Asking HR for the balance, CHASE.

34
00:02:11,525 --> 00:02:14,892
Typing the same data
again, REKEY, three times.

35
00:02:14,892 --> 00:02:18,712
The decision is JUDGE: real human
judgement, which we protect.

36
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:21,752
Finally, count.

37
00:02:21,752 --> 00:02:26,663
Eight hand-offs, twenty-five minutes of touch
time, and about four working days from start

38
00:02:26,663 --> 00:02:27,139
to end.

39
00:02:27,139 --> 00:02:30,843
Write that in the title, and
export a PNG for your report.

40
00:02:30,843 --> 00:02:35,336
That gap between minutes and days
is exactly what Session 4 works on.

